ironment": 8.2, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.68}, "rating_margin": -14.44, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.28, "baseline_margin": -10.46, "analyst_adjustment": -1.54, "key_factors": ["Strongly Republican district (R+11 partisan lean) with no Democratic baseline of support", "Mark Tedford holds a large fundraising edge ($1.8M raised, outside support) and carries Trump's endorsement", "Expert consensus is Safe/Solid R across nine raters; markets price a 93% Republican win", "Open seat removes incumbent advantage but does not threaten Republican hold"], "rationale": "The Tulsa-area seat's R+11 lean, Tedford's dominant fundraising and Trump endorsement, and a unanimous expert consensus of Safe or Solid R leave no evidence pointing toward an upset. Markets price the Republican at roughly 93%, consistent with a double-digit expected margin.", "watch": "A late Democratic-funded surge or an unusually favorable national environment could tighten the race, but no polling or spending signals suggest that is underway.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Kevin Hern (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Kevin Hern's run for Senate leaves this Tulsa-area seat open, but the district's strong Republican lean makes the GOP the heavy favorite to hold it. Mark Tedford emerged from a primary runoff against Jackson Lahmeyer, who withdrew after Trump endorsed Tedford. Tedford has raised about $1.8 million with outside support, while Democrat John Croisant has raised roughly $142,000 with modest cash on hand.\n\nNo public general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals: an R+11 partisan lean, a large financial gap, and expert ratings ranging from Likely R to Safe R, with most calling it Safe or Solid R. Prediction markets give the Republican about a 93% chance. The expected margin is a double-digit Republican win, with some residual uncertainty from the